    "lawCode": "Rpld §130 subd1 ¶ j sub¶¶ 1 & 2, amd §130, Civ Serv L; amd §207-a, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Civil Service Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A8954\n\nTITLE  OF BILL:  An act to amend the civil service law, in relation to\ncompensation, benefits and other terms and conditions of employment of\ncertain members of the security supervisors unit who are  employed  by\nthe  state  department  of correctional services and are designated as\npeace officers; to amend the state finance law,  in  relation  to  the\nemployee  benefit fund for certain members of the security supervisors\nunit; to provide for the payment of a  firearms  training  and  safety\nincentive  for  peace  officers  who  are members of the professional,\nscientific and professional services bargaining unit; to implement  an\ninterest  arbitration  award issued between the state and the employee\norganization representing certain members of the security  supervisors\nunit; to make an appropriation for the purpose of effectuating certain\nof  the  provisions  thereof;  and to repeal certain provisions of the\ncivil service law relating thereto\n\nPurpose:   This bill would implement the  provisions  of  an  interest\narbitration  award  (\"Award\")  issued  pursuant  to  Civil Service Law\n(\"CSL\") § 209(4) binding the executive branch of New  York  State  and\nthe  employee  organization (\"Council 82\") representing members of the\nSecurity Supervisors collective negotiating unit who are  employed  by\nthe  State  Department  of Correctional Services and are designated as\npeace officers pursuant to Criminal Procedure Law (\"CPL\")  §  2.10(25)\n(\"members of the unit\" or \"unit members\").\n\nThis  bill  would  also provide for the payment of a Firearms Training\nand Safety  Incentive  to  peace  officers  who  are  members  of  the\nProfessional,  Scientific  and Technical Services Unit pursuant to the\nterms of a pilot program collectively negotiated by New York State and\nthe employee  organization  representing  such  members,  pursuant  to\nArticle 14 of the CSL, to rectify an omission in a previous bill.\n\nSummary  of  Provisions:    Section  1 of this bill would repeal CSL\n130(1)(j)(1) and (2) and add new subparagraphs 1, 2 and  3  to  CSL\n130(I)(j)  to  establish salary schedules covering members of the unit\nto reflect increases in basic annual salary, effective April 1,  2005,\nApril 2006 and March 31. 2007 as set forth in § 3 of this bill.\n\nSection  2  of  this  bill  would  amend  State  Finance Law (\"SFL\")\n207-a(2-a) to provide for the continuation of employer payments to the\nemployee benefit fund established by Council 82  for  members  of  the\nunit.  Such  payments will reflect an increase in the contribution per\nunit member. effective April 1. 2006. from $25 to $30 for  those  Unit\nmembers  who were on the payroll on the last day of the payroll period\nin which March 1, 2006 fell.\n\nSection  3  of  this  bill  would  provide  for  the  compensation  of\napplicable  members  of  the  unit in accordance with the terms of the\nAward, including:\n\n* a 2.25 % salary increase, effective April 1. 2005; a 2.75  %  salary\nincrease,  effective  April  1,  2006:  and  a $3,050 salary increase.\neffective March 31, 2007  to  reflect  the  addition  of  the  uniform\nmaintenance  allowance  and security enforcement differential to basic\nannual salary:\n\n\n* advancement payments to members  of  the  unit  pursuant  to  CSL\n131(6); and\n\n* enhanced longevity step payments.\n\nThis  section  would  also  give to the Director of the Budget and the\nDirector of Employee Relations discretion to withhold all or a portion\nof a unit member's salary increase, where any such increase would  not\nbe appropriate or warranted.\n\nSection  4  of  this  bill  would  provide  additional compensation to\nmembers of the unit, in accordance with the terms  of  the  Award,  in\nrecognition  of  the  general  requirement  for full-time employees to\nassemble for briefing prior to the commencement of their duties.\n\nSection 5 of this  bill  would  continue  the  payment  of  an  annual\ncorrections clothing maintenance allowance of $1,500 to members of the\nunit.  Effective  March  31,  2007,  however,  such allowance would be\nincluded in basic annual salary of members of the unit and  no  longer\nidentified as a separate benefit.\n\nSection  6  of this bill would provide location pay for members of the\nunit whose official work station is located in New York City  and  the\ncounties of Nassau, Suffolk, Westchester, Rockland, Orange, Putnam and\nDutchess, pursuant to the terms of the Award, as follows:\n\n* Effective April 1, 2005:\n\nOrange, Putnam, Dutchess       $1,092\nNYC. Rockland, Westchester     $2,938\nNassau, Suffolk                $3,093\n\n* Effective April 1, 2006:\n\nOrange, Putnam, Dutchess       $1,126\nNYC, Rockland. Westchester     $3.026\nNassau. Suffolk                $3,093\n\nThe  location  payment  will  be  equally  divided over the 26 payroll\nperiods in each  fiscal  year  and  will  count  as  compensation  for\novertime and retirement purposes.\n\nSection 7 of this bill would provide for the payment to members of the\nunit   of  a  security  enforcement  differential  in  the  amount  of\n$850,,effective April 1, 2005, and $1,100, effective  April  1,  2006,\nEffective  March  31.  2007, such differential in the amount of $1,550\nwould be included in the basic annual salary of such  members  and  no\nlonger identified as separate benefit.\n\nSection 8 of this bill would provide for an inconvenience pay program,\neffective  April  1,  2006, which would pay $900 or $1,800 annually to\nthose unit  members  who  work  the  night  shift  or  evening  shift,\nrespectively.\n\nSection  9 of this bill would continue the annual payment of $1,500 to\nmembers of the  unit  in  recognition  of  their  command  duties  and\nresponsibilities  performed  with regard to infectious disease, mental\n\n\nhealth, crime scene control, prisoner transport and other inter-agency\nissues which arise in correctional facilities.\n\nSection  10 of this bill would provide for the continuation of a joint\ncommittee On health benefits to be funded in the amount of $6,400  for\nthe  period April 1, 2005 to March 31, 2006. and $6,400 for the period\nApril 1, 2006 to March 31, 2007. One-half of  these  amounts  will  be\nmade available to both the State and Council 82.\n\nSection  11  of  this bill would provide for the payment of a Firearms\nTraining and Safety incentive to peace officers who are members of the\nProfessional, Scientific and Technical Services Unit, pursuant to  the\nterms  of a pilot program collectively negotiated between the employee\norganization representing such members and New York State,\n\nSection 12 of-this bill would make the appropriations contained in the\nbill  available  for  the  payment  and   publication   of   grievance\narbitration settlements and awards.\n\nSection 13 of this bill would provide that no increase in compensation\nset  forth  in  the  bill  would be payable to an individual until and\nunless he or she is a member of unit, employed by the State Department\nof Correctional Services and designated as a peace officer pursuant to\nCPL § 2.10(25).\n\nSection 14 of the bill would continue to exempt the salaries of  newly\nhired  unit members from the imposition of \"deferred payment\" pursuant\nto SFL § 200(2-a).\n\nSection 15 of the bill would set the date upon which incumbent members\nof the unit would receive the salary increases provided by the Award.\n\nSection 16 of the bill  would  provide  that  until  payment  is  made\npursuant  to  this  bill,  members  of  the  unit will receive payment\notherwise payable in their respective positions.   When  basic  annual\nsalaries  are  first paid pursuant to this bill, such members shall be\nentitled to a lump sum payment for the difference between the salaries\nto which they were entitled since April 1, 2005 and  the  compensation\nactually received.\n\nSection  17  of  the  bill  would authorize the Comptroller to pay any\namounts  required  by  the  bill.  To   the   extent   that   existing\nappropriations  available  to  any  State  department  or  agency  are\ninsufficient to accomplish the purposes set forth in this section, the\nDirector of the Budget would be authorized to allocate to the  various\ndepartments and agencies the amounts necessary to make such payments.\n\nSection  18  of the bill would appropriate approximately $17.2 million\nin the General Fund to pay for personal and non-personal services  for\nmembers  of  the  unit as provided in this bill and in accordance with\nthe terms of the Award.\n\nSection 19 of the bill would provide that it take  effect  immediately\nand be deemed to have been in full force and effect on and after April\n1,  2005,  except  for § 11 of the bill, which would be deemed to have\nbeen in full force and effect on and after April 1, 2008.\n\n\nExisting Law:  Chapter 4 of the Laws of 2007 sets forth several  terms\nand  conditions  of employment relating to compensation for members of\nthe unit from April 1, 2003 to March 31, 2005.  In  addition,  Chapter\n375  of  the  Laws  of  2007 sets forth several terms of conditions of\nemployment  not  directly relating to compensation for the time period\nbeginning April 1, 2003 and ending March 31. 2007.\n\nCSL § 130(1)(j) sets forth the current salary and longevity  schedules\nfor members of the unit.\n\nCSL § 134(5) provides for the current pre-shift briefing payment.\n\nChapter  114  of  the  Laws  of  2008  implements  the provisions of a\ncollective bargaining agreement between the Executive  Branch  of  the\nState  of  New  York  and  the  employee  organization that represents\nmembers of the Professional, Scientific and Technical  Services  Unit,\nfor period beginning April 2, 2007 and ending April 1, 2011.\n\nPrior Legislative History: None.\n\nStatement  in Support:   This bill is necessary to implement the terms\nof an interest arbitration award issued  pursuant  to  CSL  §  209(4),\nbinding   the   State  of  New  York  and  the  employee  organization\nrepresenting  members   of   the   Security   Supervisors   collective\nnegotiating unit.\n\nSection  11  of  this  bill is necessary to implement a provision of a\ncollective bargaining agreement between the State of New York and  the\nemployee  organization  that  represents  members of the Professional,\nScientific  and  Technical  Services  Unit,  which  was  inadvertently\nomitted from a previous bill.\n\nBudget  Implications:    This  bill  would  provide for appropriations\ntotaling approximately $17.27 million, all  of  which  is  within  the\nGeneral  Fund,  to  pay for the cost of the interest arbitration award\ncovering the period April 1, 2005  to  March  31,  2007,  The  2009-10\nfinancial plan accommodates the costs associated with this bill.\n\nEffective Date:  This bill would take effect immediately and be deemed\nto  have  been  in  full  force and effect on and after April 1, 2005,\nexcept for § 1.1 of the bill, which would be deemed to have.. been  in\nfull force and effect on and after April 1, 2008.\n\n",
